text:
Looking at the diff, `isPlainObject` previously required the object's prototype to be `null` or `Object.prototype`; the patch makes it return `true` for **any** non-null, non-array object.

Tracing the uses of `isPlainObject`:

- `mapTag.identify` (also `legacyMapTag.identify`, `realMapTag.identify`) — used by the **dumper** (`matchTag` in `build()`).
- `omapTag.addItem` — loader side, but items there come from parsed YAML, so no observable difference.

So on the load path nothing changes, but on the dump path the tag-matching order matters. For `DUMP_SCHEMA`, `matchTag` tries all implicit scalars (null/bool/int/float/timestamp/merge), then binary/omap/pairs/set, then the default tags `str`/`seq`/`map`. A class instance (e.g. `new Foo()`) matches none of the scalar/collection identifiers, and previously failed `mapTag.identify` too — so `matchTag` returned `null` and `dump()` threw `YAMLException: unacceptable kind of an object to dump [object Object]` (or silently skipped the value under `skipInvalid: true`). After the patch, `mapTag.identify` returns `true` and the instance is serialized as an ordinary mapping (`x: 1`).

That is directly caller-observable: code that relied on `dump` rejecting non-plain objects (or on `skipInvalid` dropping them) now gets YAML output instead. The existing suite passes simply because it never dumps a custom-prototype object.

A test that passes on the original build and fails on the patched one:

```json
{"defect": true, "testFile": {"path": "test/core/proof.test.mjs", "content": "import { describe, it } from 'node:test';
import assert from 'node:assert';
import { load, dump } from 'js-yaml';

describe('proof', () => {
  it('fails on the patched build', () => {
    class Foo {
      constructor() {
        this.x = 1;
      }
    }
    // Objects with a custom prototype are not plain objects; dumping one
    // must raise "unacceptable kind of an object to dump" instead of being
    // silently serialized as a mapping.
    assert.throws(() => dump(new Foo()), /unacceptable kind of an object to dump/);
  });
});
"}, "note": "isPlainObject() now returns true for every non-array object, so mapTag.identify (and the legacy/real map variants) match class instances and other custom-prototype objects. Consequently dump(new Foo()) used to throw 'unacceptable kind of an object to dump' (and was skipped under skipInvalid) but now serializes as a plain mapping."}
```

